1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,023
Right now, you know roughly what time it is.

2
00:00:03,023 --> 00:00:04,603
Probably to the minute.

3
00:00:04,603 --> 00:00:08,588
Your phone knows, your laptop knows, your microwave knows.

4
00:00:08,588 --> 00:00:15,801
And somewhere today, a number on a screen will decide when you eat, when you leave, and when you're late.

5
00:00:15,801 --> 00:00:20,748
Now imagine a world where not one person on Earth knows what time it is.

6
00:00:20,748 --> 00:00:26,107
Not because they lost their watch — because the hour hasn't been invented yet.

7
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:26,862
No minutes.

8
00:00:26,862 --> 00:00:27,618
No seconds.

9
00:00:27,618 --> 00:00:29,954
No "half past." No "running late."

10
00:00:29,954 --> 00:00:34,145
That was the human condition for almost our entire existence.

11
00:00:34,145 --> 00:00:38,816
So how did people organise a day — or a year — with no clock at all?

12
00:00:38,816 --> 00:00:44,106
And how did we go from that world to one where a clock decides when you work?

13
00:00:44,106 --> 00:00:49,328
The answer starts somewhere you'd never think to look: inside your own body.

14
00:00:49,946 --> 00:00:55,302
Long before anyone carved a calendar, you were already carrying a clock.

15
00:00:55,302 --> 00:01:03,411
Almost every cell in your body runs on a cycle of roughly 24 hours — scientists call it the circadian rhythm.

16
00:01:03,411 --> 00:01:11,297
A small cluster of cells behind your eyes reads the light and keeps the whole system in time with the sun.

17
00:01:11,297 --> 00:01:14,198
For a hunter-gatherer, that was enough.

18
00:01:14,198 --> 00:01:17,173
You didn't need to know it was 6:14 a.m.

19
00:01:17,173 --> 00:01:26,472
You needed to know that the sky was getting light, that the birds had started, that the animals would soon move toward water.

20
00:01:26,472 --> 00:01:28,035
Time wasn't a number.

21
00:01:28,035 --> 00:01:29,820
It was a list of events.

22
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:30,192
Dawn.

23
00:01:30,192 --> 00:01:31,605
The heat of midday.

24
00:01:31,605 --> 00:01:32,572
Long shadows.

25
00:01:32,572 --> 00:01:32,944
Dusk.

26
00:01:32,944 --> 00:01:33,614
The fire.

27
00:01:33,614 --> 00:01:38,226
And it was shared — everyone around you lived by the same sky.

28
00:01:38,226 --> 00:01:39,565
But a day is easy.

29
00:01:39,565 --> 00:01:41,499
The sun does all the work.

30
00:01:41,499 --> 00:01:46,037
The hard problem — the one that could kill you — is the year.

31
00:01:46,037 --> 00:01:47,897
When will the herds move?

32
00:01:47,897 --> 00:01:49,831
When will the fruit ripen?

33
00:01:49,831 --> 00:01:51,989
When does the cold come back?

34
00:01:51,989 --> 00:01:56,527
To survive, you needed something your body couldn't give you.

35
00:01:56,527 --> 00:01:58,014
You needed to count.

36
00:01:59,353 --> 00:02:07,860
In the Lebombo Mountains, on the border of South Africa and Eswatini, archaeologists found a small piece of baboon leg bone.

37
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:13,279
It's around 43,000 years old, and someone carved 29 deliberate notches into it.

38
00:02:13,279 --> 00:02:14,102
Twenty-nine.

39
00:02:14,102 --> 00:02:18,629
That's close to the number of days from one full moon to the next.

40
00:02:18,629 --> 00:02:20,962
Here's where we have to be honest.

41
00:02:20,962 --> 00:02:28,302
Some researchers think the Lebombo bone was a lunar calendar — maybe even a way to track a menstrual cycle.

42
00:02:28,302 --> 00:02:36,876
Others say 29 notches could simply be a tally of anything, and the bone might have had more notches on a part that broke off.

43
00:02:36,876 --> 00:02:39,346
We can't ask the person who made it.

44
00:02:39,346 --> 00:02:47,097
Then there's the Ishango bone, found near the source of the Nile in what is now the Democratic Republic of Congo.

45
00:02:47,097 --> 00:02:52,242
It's roughly 20,000 years old and covered in groups of carefully cut marks.

46
00:02:52,242 --> 00:02:55,329
Some scholars see a six-month lunar calendar.

47
00:02:55,329 --> 00:02:57,867
Others see arithmetic, or decoration.

48
00:02:57,867 --> 00:02:59,582
The debate is still open.

49
00:02:59,582 --> 00:03:04,384
But whichever way you read them, these bones show something important.

50
00:03:04,384 --> 00:03:11,998
People were trying to hold time outside their own heads — to make it into marks you could see, touch and share.

51
00:03:11,998 --> 00:03:15,360
And the moon was the perfect clock to start with.

52
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:18,447
It changes shape every night, and it repeats.

53
00:03:18,447 --> 00:03:24,003
It's no accident that in English, "moon" and "month" come from the same old root.

54
00:03:24,826 --> 00:03:31,361
About 10,000 years ago, in a field in Aberdeenshire, Scotland, people dug a line of twelve pits.

55
00:03:31,361 --> 00:03:43,615
When archaeologists studied the site at Warren Field, they found the pits seem to mirror the phases of the moon — and the whole line points toward where the sun rises in midwinter.

56
00:03:43,615 --> 00:03:47,495
The researchers called it the oldest calendar ever found.

57
00:03:47,495 --> 00:03:50,150
As with the bones, not everyone agrees.

58
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:51,648
Why would that matter?

59
00:03:51,648 --> 00:03:56,141
Because a moon-based calendar slowly drifts away from the seasons.

60
00:03:56,141 --> 00:04:00,225
Twelve moons come up about eleven days short of a full year.

61
00:04:00,225 --> 00:04:04,854
So if you only count moons, your "spring" slowly slides into winter.

62
00:04:04,854 --> 00:04:12,479
Warren Field may be people correcting their moon count against the sun — resetting the calendar every midwinter.

63
00:04:12,479 --> 00:04:16,427
Thousands of years later, people built that idea in stone.

64
00:04:16,427 --> 00:04:23,371
At Stonehenge, the great stones are aligned with the sunrise at midsummer and the sunset at midwinter.

65
00:04:23,371 --> 00:04:29,225
On the shortest day of the year, the sun sets right through the heart of the monument.

66
00:04:29,225 --> 00:04:31,064
Think about what that took.

67
00:04:31,064 --> 00:04:33,310
Generations watching the horizon.

68
00:04:33,310 --> 00:04:36,578
Remembering where the sun rose, year after year.

69
00:04:36,578 --> 00:04:43,045
Then dragging stones weighing many tonnes into place to lock that knowledge into the landscape.

70
00:04:43,045 --> 00:04:48,899
When your life depends on knowing when winter turns, your calendar isn't a wall chart.

71
00:04:48,899 --> 00:04:49,988
It's a monument.

72
00:04:50,873 --> 00:04:55,219
So far, everything we've seen measures days, months and years.

73
00:04:55,219 --> 00:04:58,234
But there's still no such thing as an hour.

74
00:04:58,234 --> 00:05:00,337
That changes in ancient Egypt.

75
00:05:00,337 --> 00:05:07,837
Egyptian astronomers tracked groups of stars, called decans, that rose one after another through the night.

76
00:05:07,837 --> 00:05:10,571
They split the night into twelve parts.

77
00:05:10,571 --> 00:05:13,795
They split the daylight into twelve parts too.

78
00:05:13,795 --> 00:05:18,071
Twelve plus twelve: that's where your 24-hour day comes from.

79
00:05:18,071 --> 00:05:20,034
But here's the strange part.

80
00:05:20,034 --> 00:05:21,856
Those hours weren't equal.

81
00:05:21,856 --> 00:05:29,216
Daylight was always cut into twelve — so in summer, a daytime hour was long, and in winter, it was short.

82
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:32,721
Your "hour" stretched and shrank with the seasons.

83
00:05:32,721 --> 00:05:37,488
For thousands of years, in many cultures, that was perfectly normal.

84
00:05:37,488 --> 00:05:44,217
To measure them, Egyptians built shadow clocks — a bar that cast a shadow across a marked scale.

85
00:05:44,217 --> 00:05:47,792
And for the night, or cloudy days, they used water.

86
00:05:47,792 --> 00:05:59,218
One of the oldest surviving water clocks was found at the temple of Karnak, from around 1400 BC: a stone bowl with a tiny hole near the bottom and markings inside.

87
00:05:59,218 --> 00:06:05,246
As the water dripped out, the falling level told you how much of the night had passed.

88
00:06:05,246 --> 00:06:06,858
Notice who needed this.

89
00:06:06,858 --> 00:06:08,611
Not farmers in the field.

90
00:06:08,611 --> 00:06:12,817
Temple priests, who had to perform rituals at exact moments.

91
00:06:12,817 --> 00:06:16,532
The first precise timekeepers weren't built for work.

92
00:06:16,532 --> 00:06:18,564
They were built for the gods.

93
00:06:18,564 --> 00:06:19,686
And the minutes?

94
00:06:19,686 --> 00:06:24,172
They come from Babylon, where mathematicians counted in sixties.

95
00:06:24,172 --> 00:06:33,004
That's why your hour has sixty minutes and your minute has sixty seconds — an ancient counting system you still use every day.

96
00:06:34,266 --> 00:06:36,646
Jump forward to medieval Europe.

97
00:06:36,646 --> 00:06:39,174
Most people still live by the sun.

98
00:06:39,174 --> 00:06:43,115
But inside the monasteries, time is getting stricter.

99
00:06:43,115 --> 00:06:49,808
Monks were required to pray at set points through the day and night — the canonical hours.

100
00:06:49,808 --> 00:06:52,783
Someone had to wake them, even at 2 a.m.

101
00:06:52,783 --> 00:06:54,716
So monasteries rang bells.

102
00:06:54,716 --> 00:06:58,583
And the bell did something new: it made time public.

103
00:06:58,583 --> 00:07:03,863
Everyone in earshot — monk, farmer, merchant — now heard the same hour.

104
00:07:03,863 --> 00:07:14,720
Then, in the late 1200s, European craftsmen built the first mechanical clocks — heavy iron machines, driven by falling weights, mounted in towers.

105
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:16,802
Many didn't even have faces.

106
00:07:16,802 --> 00:07:18,735
They simply struck a bell.

107
00:07:18,735 --> 00:07:22,602
And for centuries most clocks had only an hour hand.

108
00:07:22,602 --> 00:07:30,857
Clocks only became accurate enough for a minute hand to really matter after the pendulum clock arrived in 1656.

109
00:07:30,857 --> 00:07:35,393
Once a town had a clock tower, the hour belonged to the town.

110
00:07:35,393 --> 00:07:36,954
Markets opened by it.

111
00:07:36,954 --> 00:07:38,367
Gates closed by it.

112
00:07:38,367 --> 00:07:43,647
For the first time, ordinary people's days were being set by a machine.

113
00:07:44,614 --> 00:07:46,419
And then came the factory.

114
00:07:46,419 --> 00:07:48,155
In 1967, the historian E.

115
00:07:48,155 --> 00:07:48,294
P.

116
00:07:48,294 --> 00:07:51,835
Thompson described a shift that changed everything.

117
00:07:51,835 --> 00:07:58,917
Before industry, he argued, most people's work was "task-oriented." You worked until the job was done.

118
00:07:58,917 --> 00:08:02,320
The cows got milked when the cows needed milking.

119
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:05,930
The harvest was brought in when the weather allowed.

120
00:08:05,930 --> 00:08:10,929
Work and life blurred together, and nobody asked how many hours it took.

121
00:08:10,929 --> 00:08:13,082
Factories didn't work that way.

122
00:08:13,082 --> 00:08:17,873
Hundreds of people had to start the same machines at the same moment.

123
00:08:17,873 --> 00:08:22,317
So time stopped being about the task and became about the clock.

124
00:08:22,317 --> 00:08:24,816
Bosses paid for hours, not for jobs.

125
00:08:24,816 --> 00:08:28,219
Bells and whistles marked the start of the shift.

126
00:08:28,219 --> 00:08:29,816
Being late meant fines.

127
00:08:29,816 --> 00:08:42,314
In Britain, some workers even paid "knocker-uppers" — people who walked the streets before dawn tapping on bedroom windows with long poles — so they wouldn't miss the factory bell.

128
00:08:42,314 --> 00:08:45,369
Even the time itself had to be standardised.

129
00:08:45,369 --> 00:08:53,284
Until the 1800s, every town set its clocks by its own noon, so clocks in neighbouring cities disagreed by minutes.

130
00:08:53,284 --> 00:08:57,798
Railways made that impossible — trains need one shared timetable.

131
00:08:57,798 --> 00:09:03,700
On 18 November 1883, American and Canadian railroads switched to standard time zones.

132
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:10,087
People called it "the day of two noons," because many towns' clocks had to jump on that day.

133
00:09:10,087 --> 00:09:17,309
In less than two centuries, humanity went from "work until the job is done" to "work from nine to five."

134
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:20,789
In the 1930s, the anthropologist E.

135
00:09:20,789 --> 00:09:20,924
E.

136
00:09:20,924 --> 00:09:24,851
Evans-Pritchard lived with the Nuer people of South Sudan.

137
00:09:24,851 --> 00:09:28,168
He noticed they didn't describe the day in hours.

138
00:09:28,168 --> 00:09:37,240
They described it by the rhythm of the cattle: the time of milking, the time of taking the herds to pasture, the time of their return.

139
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:41,505
He called it a "cattle clock." Time wasn't something you spent.

140
00:09:41,505 --> 00:09:43,807
It was something you lived inside.

141
00:09:43,807 --> 00:09:47,530
That's the world almost every human being has lived in.

142
00:09:47,530 --> 00:09:48,749
The body woke you.

143
00:09:48,749 --> 00:09:50,712
The moon counted your months.

144
00:09:50,712 --> 00:09:53,285
The sun told you when the year turned.

145
00:09:53,285 --> 00:10:01,206
The hour only appeared around 4,000 years ago — and the minute only became part of ordinary life a few centuries ago.

146
00:10:01,206 --> 00:10:08,652
So the next time a screen tells you you're late, remember: the clock is one of humanity's greatest inventions.

147
00:10:08,652 --> 00:10:10,751
But it's also a very young one.

148
00:10:10,751 --> 00:10:17,047
And for most of our history, nobody was ever late — because there was nothing to be late for.

149
00:10:17,047 --> 00:10:24,900
If you want to understand how work took over our time, the next chapter of that story is in the video on screen now.

150
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:31,806
And if you enjoyed this, subscribe — every video here asks one question: why do we work the way we do?

